Answer the window manager's _NET_WM_SYNC_REQUEST on X11, so a resize draws in step with it.

During an interactive resize, a compositing window manager such as mutter sends the window a
_NET_WM_SYNC_REQUEST client message carrying a serial, then sets the new size, and holds the
window's contents back until the counter the window published in _NET_WM_SYNC_REQUEST_COUNTER
reaches that serial, or until its own timeout of one second. It sends no request for a maximise or
a restore. A window that publishes no counter is shown at once, with the old frame stretched or the
new area unpainted.

The window now lists _NET_WM_SYNC_REQUEST in WM_PROTOCOLS, creates an XSync counter and publishes
it. The request's serial is stored when the message arrives. The counter is set to it in one of
three places: after the buffer swap of the first frame drawn at the new size, at once when the
configure that follows keeps the size and no size change came since the request (a move, or a
request for the size the window has), and after 100 ms in process_events when no configure or no
frame comes, as for a minimized window. A newer request replaces an unanswered one, so the counter
always ends at the newest serial. A destroyed window destroys its counter first.

Only the GL path declares the handshake, since the buffer swap is where its frame is counted; the
Vulkan path keeps the protocol list it has. swap_buffers() reads two atomic counts, of pending
requests and of size changes not yet drawn, and takes no lock when both are 0, so the idle frame
path is unchanged. The GL managers gain a const getter for the window they swap.

The patch adds sync.h (libXext 1.3.5) and syncconst.h (xorgproto 2024.1) to
thirdparty/linuxbsd_headers, names them in its README, and adds XSyncQueryExtension,
XSyncInitialize, XSyncCreateCounter, XSyncSetCounter and XSyncDestroyCounter to the libXext wrapper
in the generated format. Those five entries are added by hand, and a comment in both wrapper files
says so. Tekton applies the patch to its pinned build of the engine.

